"""A chain mapper that READS A TABLE OTHER THAN THE ONE THAT TRIGGERED IT.

This file is a reference implementation. It is meant to be copied, so it carries the
reasoning with it: every rule below names the failure it prevents, and the ones that
came from a real incident say so. A sample that showed only the happy path would be
copied too, and the copy would carry all the defects.

`server/mappers/` is gitignored; only `.sample` files are tracked. Copy this to a real
`.py` beside it, rename the function, and declare it in `config/chain_rules.json`.


WHEN THIS IS THE WRONG TOOL - READ THIS BEFORE THE CODE
=======================================================
If what you want is "show a column that lives on another table", STOP. That is a
VIRTUAL JOIN (`config/virtual_join_rules.json`, executed by `virtual_join_executor`),
and it is better than this file at that job in every respect:

  * It is a DECLARATION, not code. An operator can add one without a deploy.
  * It copies nothing, so the announced value follows the right table. Correct the
    right table and every row that shows the column is correct on the next read.
  * It is one LEFT JOIN per rule per page, and the loader REFUSES a declaration whose
    right side lacks a UNIQUE index covering the join key, so it cannot fan out.
  * Its values are already announced in `GET /tables/{t}/schema`, already filterable
    and searchable, and already come out of the CSV extract. A virtual column is NOT
    display-only, so "I need to filter/export it" is not a reason to write a mapper.
    (Sorting is the one thing it does not do - user ruling, deliberately out of scope.)

A mapper lookup earns its place only when at least one of these is true:

  1. THE VALUE MUST BE COMPUTED. `expose` names a column and hands it over verbatim.
     There is no expression, and there is no aggregation - the loader's own refusal
     code `fanout_declared` says the aggregate shape is not implemented. Arithmetic,
     a coordinate transform, a chosen-among-several: none of that is expressible in a
     declaration, so it has to be code.

  2. THE VALUE MUST BE FROZEN AT DERIVATION TIME. This is the sharper of the two and
     it is the reason most such mappers exist. A virtual join is LIVE by construction:
     it re-reads the right table every time anybody looks. That is usually what you
     want, and it is exactly what you must not have when the derived row is a RECORD
     of what was true when it was made. If a later correction on the reference table
     may not retroactively rewrite a finished record, the value has to be stored, and
     storing it means writing it, and writing it means a mapper.
     Note the consequence you are accepting: a frozen value goes stale on purpose, so
     the derived row must also record WHICH reference value it froze, or nobody can
     later tell a stale-by-design cell from a wrong one.

  3. THE RIGHT SIDE IS NOT UNIQUE BY THE JOIN KEY AND CANNOT BE MADE SO. The virtual
     join refuses such a declaration outright rather than fanning out. If the
     duplicates are real (a history table keyed by time, say) then choosing among them
     is a decision, and a decision is code.

  4. THE OUTPUT IS ROWS THAT DO NOT EXIST YET. A virtual join can only decorate rows
     the left table already has. A chain writes into a target table, so it can create
     rows with no left-hand counterpart at all.

If none of those apply, delete this file and write a declaration.

A correction to a plausible-sounding version of the rule: it is NOT "computed or frozen
versus merely displayed". Virtual columns are not merely displayed - they filter,
search and export. The line is verbatim-and-live versus computed-or-frozen.


THE CONTRACT, AS THE WORKER ACTUALLY IMPLEMENTS IT
==================================================
Verified against `server/chain_ingestion_worker.py` at source.

  CALL          `execute_custom_mapper` imports `mapper_module`, gets
                `mapper_function`, and calls `f(db, payload, rule=rule)` when the
                function declares a `rule` parameter (or `**kwargs`), else
                `f(db, payload)`. Declaring `rule=None` as this file does is therefore
                compatible with both call paths.

  PAYLOAD       With `is_batch: true` the second argument is a LIST of outbox payload
                dicts - every CREATE/EDIT event of the trigger table in one
                transaction group, minus the ones this chain wrote itself
                (`source_name == "chain_ingestion"`, the circular-loop filter).
                With `is_batch: false` it is a SINGLE dict and the mapper is called
                once per event.
                Each payload is `{"row_id", "business_key", "data", "transaction_id",
                "updated_by", "source_name", "timestamp"}`, where `data` maps every
                declared column of the committed row to `{"value", "is_overwrite",
                "updated_by"}`. `row_id`, `business_key_val` and the timestamps are
                lifted out of `data` and carried beside it.

  RETURN        A dict. The worker reads `["updates"]` and nothing else, extending its
                per-target list with it. A falsy return, a non-dict, or a dict without
                a non-empty `updates` is ignored silently. Each item is a
                `schemas.GeneralUpdateItem`: `{"business_key_val" | "row_id",
                "updates": {column: value}, "source_name", "updated_by"}`.

  DISAGREEMENT  The other samples in this directory return `"silent": False` alongside
                `updates`. THE WORKER DOES NOT READ IT. It builds the
                `GeneralUpdateBatch` itself and hardcodes `silent=False`, so a mapper
                that returned `"silent": True` would be broadcast anyway. The key is
                dead. This file omits it rather than teach a control that does not
                exist.

  SESSION       The worker owns it. One session is opened per polling batch and closed
                at the end of that batch; every transaction group in the batch shares
                it. The worker is the only committer: it commits the outbox bookkeeping
                after a group succeeds, commits the retry bookkeeping after a group
                fails, and `crud.apply_batch_updates` commits the target write.

  FAILURE       An exception out of a mapper fails the whole transaction group. The
                worker rolls back, increments `retry_count` on every event in the
                group, and quarantines them as FAILED once the retry limit is reached.
                So raising is a legitimate thing to do - it is how you say "this cannot
                be derived and should be retried". Returning empty is how you say
                "nothing to derive here". Do not confuse them: an empty return on a
                real failure means the group is marked SUCCESS and never looked at
                again.


THE RULES THIS FILE EXISTS TO DEMONSTRATE
=========================================
  A. READ, NEVER COMMIT.
  B. CONTAIN A FAILED READ IN A SAVEPOINT.
  C. BATCH THE LOOKUP.
  D. ABSENCE IS A VALUE.
  E. DO NOT RE-READ THE TRIGGER ROW.

Each is argued at the code that implements it.
"""

import logging

from sqlalchemy.orm import Session

# The degradation vocabulary is a CLOSED SET and it is defined once. Importing it here
# rather than retyping the strings is the point: `config_resolve_report.entry` raises
# on a word outside `REASONS`, so the operator-facing surface cannot drift from what a
# derived row says. Do not invent a new word for "we did not find it" - there already
# is one, and a second spelling means the grid and the config report disagree about the
# same fact.
import config_resolve_report as vocab

logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)


# --- Named outcomes -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `not_declared`        the reference record is not there, or is there and blank.
# `mapping_unavailable` the reference is declared but could not be read: the statement
#                       failed, or the value came back in a shape this rule cannot use.
#
# The split is not cosmetic. It is the difference between two REPAIRS - "go create the
# reference row" versus "go look at why the read failed" - and `bonding_plan` records
# the same reasoning where it picks between the same words: folding them into one sends
# the operator to fill in a declaration that already exists.
REASON_ABSENT = vocab.REASON_NOT_DECLARED
REASON_UNREADABLE = vocab.REASON_MAPPING_UNAVAILABLE

# Refusals abort the whole call instead of degrading a row at a time. They are NAMED
# because an empty `updates` list is indistinguishable from "nothing to do", and an
# operator cannot act on a silence. Same posture as `dt_map_derivation.DerivationRefused`.
REFUSE_LOOKUP_TABLE_UNKNOWN = "lookup_table_unknown"
REFUSE_LOOKUP_KEY_UNDECLARED = "lookup_key_undeclared"
REFUSE_TARGET_COLUMN_UNDECLARED = "target_column_undeclared"

# Keys per statement. The same value the rest of the system uses for `IN (...)`
# chunking (`dt_map_derivation.CHUNK`, `virtual_join_executor.CHUNK_SIZE`,
# `enrichment_candidates.CHUNK_SIZE`). Same operation, same constant.
LOOKUP_CHUNK = 1000


class LookupRefused(Exception):
    """Raised with a NAMED code when the call cannot proceed at all."""

    def __init__(self, code: str, detail: str):
        self.code = code
        self.detail = detail
        super().__init__("%s: %s" % (code, detail))


# --- Declarations ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# These are DEFAULTS, not constants, and every one of them is overridable per rule.
# A mapper that reads `unit_weight` because someone typed `unit_weight` is correct in
# exactly the environment it was typed in - the dev database and production disagree
# about table shapes here, which is why `dt_map_mapper` takes the same posture.
DEFAULT_LOOKUP_TABLE = "material_master"
DEFAULT_LOOKUP_KEY = ({"left": "part_no", "right": "part_no"},)
DEFAULT_FACTOR_COLUMNS = ("unit_weight", "pack_size")
DEFAULT_TARGET_KEY_COLUMN = "part_no"
DEFAULT_COMPUTED_COLUMN = "pack_weight"
DEFAULT_FROZEN_COLUMNS = ("unit_weight_used", "pack_size_used")
DEFAULT_STATUS_COLUMN = "lookup_status"


def _rule_str(rule, key, default):
    return str((rule or {}).get(key) or default)


def _rule_tuple(rule, key, default):
    declared = (rule or {}).get(key)
    return tuple(declared) if declared else tuple(default)


def _lookup_pairs(rule) -> list:
    """[(column on the trigger row, column on the lookup table)].

    Spelled `left`/`right` because that is what a virtual-join `join_key` is spelled,
    and an operator who has declared one of those should not have to learn a second
    vocabulary to declare this.
    """
    declared = (rule or {}).get("lookup_key") or DEFAULT_LOOKUP_KEY
    pairs = []
    for pair in declared:
        left, right = pair.get("left"), pair.get("right")
        if left and right:
            pairs.append((str(left), str(right)))
    if not pairs:
        raise LookupRefused(
            REFUSE_LOOKUP_KEY_UNDECLARED,
            "rule declares no usable `lookup_key`; without one the reference read "
            "would either match nothing or match everything, and those two produce "
            "the same empty-looking result for opposite reasons.")
    return pairs


def _require_declared_target_columns(target_table: str, columns) -> None:
    """RULE D, first half. An undeclared column is DROPPED, not rejected.

    `crud.apply_batch_updates` drops a column that the target does not declare in
    `column_types` and warns once per (table, column) per process, and the write still
    succeeds for the rest. That is fine for a value column and fatal for the STATUS
    column: the row would land carrying everything the mapper knew and nothing about
    what it did not, and it would look complete. A derived row that cannot say what it
    does not know must not be written at all, so this refuses up front rather than
    discovering it in a log line nobody reads.
    """
    from database import crud

    declared = (crud.TABLE_CONFIG.get(target_table) or {}).get("column_types") or {}
    missing = [c for c in columns if c and c not in declared]
    if missing:
        raise LookupRefused(
            REFUSE_TARGET_COLUMN_UNDECLARED,
            "target '%s' does not declare %r in column_types, so those cells would be "
            "silently dropped from the write. Declare them in table_config.json."
            % (target_table, missing))


# --- RULE E: the trigger row is already in your hand ------------------------------

def _flatten(payload: dict) -> dict:
    """The committed trigger row, taken from the event. NO QUERY.

    The payload's `data` carries EVERY declared column of the row as it was when the
    write committed, not just the changed ones, so a re-read buys nothing. It also
    costs something twice over: a statement per row that the batching below exists to
    avoid, and a chance to read a DIFFERENT version. The event is a snapshot of one
    committed state; by the time the chain worker wakes, that row may have been edited
    again. Re-reading mixes two versions of the same row into one derived record and
    nothing downstream can tell.

    `row_id` and `business_key` are lifted out of `data` by the event writer, so they
    are read from the top level here. (`mappers.utils.payloads_to_df` keeps `row_id`
    but not `business_key` - one reason this file stays on plain dicts. The other is
    that a DataFrame turns an absent cell into NaN, and NaN is not blank to
    `crud.is_blank_value`: it would be written out as the text "nan".)
    """
    flat = {"row_id": payload.get("row_id"), "business_key": payload.get("business_key")}
    for column, cell in (payload.get("data") or {}).items():
        if isinstance(cell, dict) and "value" in cell:
            flat[column] = cell["value"]
        else:
            flat[column] = cell
    return flat


# --- RULE B: contain a failed read -------------------------------------------------

def _isolated_fetch(db, query):
    """Run ONE reference statement inside a SAVEPOINT. Same shape, same reason, as
    `enrichment_config._isolated_execute`.

    On PostgreSQL a failed statement ABORTS the enclosing transaction. Every later
    statement on that connection then raises, and - this is what makes it silent -
    `COMMIT` on an aborted transaction RETURNS NORMALLY while the server converts it to
    a ROLLBACK. Catching the driver error is therefore NOT containment; the session is
    already dead and neither the caller nor the log can tell.

    This is a live defect in this codebase, not a hypothetical: `bonding_plan.py`
    catches a failed confirmation lookup and degrades honestly, but never calls
    `db.rollback()`, so on PostgreSQL the caller dies one query later with an error
    that names the wrong statement. Measured 2026-07-30 against the live database:
    a bad SELECT poisons the session, the next SELECT raises, `db.commit()` returns
    normally having rolled back, and the same bad SELECT inside a SAVEPOINT leaves the
    next SELECT working.

    A mapper must never call `db.rollback()` instead: the session belongs to the
    worker, and a top-level rollback would discard the bookkeeping of every group
    processed earlier in the same batch. A SAVEPOINT rolls back exactly this read and
    nothing else. That is the whole reason the containment is scoped here rather than
    at the caller.

    Cost is an extra round trip to open the savepoint and another to release it. That
    is noise beside an indexed lookup, and the alternative price was a chain worker
    that replayed the same group forever because its `processed_chain=True` commit was
    quietly turned into a rollback.

    Rolls back and RE-RAISES. Containment and degradation are different jobs: this
    function keeps the session alive, and the caller decides what the row should say.
    """
    nested = db.begin_nested()
    try:
        rows = query.all()
    except Exception:
        nested.rollback()
        raise
    nested.commit()
    return rows


# --- RULE C: one statement per chunk, never one per row ---------------------------

def load_reference(db, table: str, key_columns: list, want_columns: list, keys) -> tuple:
    """(key tuple) -> {column: value}, plus the set of keys whose read FAILED.

    Same shape as `dt_map_derivation.load_attribution`, which is the existing
    implementation of this operation. It is written out here rather than imported
    because that module pulls in the dt_map declarations and `map_overlay` with it; if
    your mapper belongs to that family, import it instead of copying this.

    WHEN THE SHAPE MATTERS. Below one chunk of DISTINCT keys the two shapes cost the
    same. Above it, a per-row lookup costs one round trip per row while this costs one
    per chunk, and the gap grows linearly from there. Note DISTINCT: a chain batch is
    as large as the ingestion transaction that produced it - a whole file, in the
    watcher's case - and a file's rows usually point at far fewer reference records
    than there are rows, so the de-duplication below is often the larger win.

    The failed keys come back as a set rather than as an exception because a chunk
    that fails is a chunk's worth of not-knowing, not a reason to throw away the
    chunks that succeeded.
    """
    from database import models

    model = models.DYNAMIC_TABLES.get(table)
    if model is None:
        raise LookupRefused(
            REFUSE_LOOKUP_TABLE_UNKNOWN,
            "lookup table '%s' has no dynamic model; it is not declared in "
            "table_config.json, or the worker has not reloaded since it was."
            % table)

    key_attrs = [getattr(model, c, None) for c in key_columns]
    absent_keys = [c for c, a in zip(key_columns, key_attrs) if a is None]
    if absent_keys:
        raise LookupRefused(
            REFUSE_LOOKUP_KEY_UNDECLARED,
            "lookup table '%s' has no column(s) %r to join on." % (table, absent_keys))

    want_attrs = [(c, getattr(model, c, None)) for c in want_columns]
    absent_wanted = [c for c, a in want_attrs if a is None]
    if absent_wanted:
        raise LookupRefused(
            REFUSE_LOOKUP_TABLE_UNKNOWN,
            "lookup table '%s' does not carry %r; a rule that reads a column the "
            "table does not have is a config error, not a per-row absence."
            % (table, absent_wanted))

    index, unreadable = {}, set()
    ordered = [k for k in keys if k is not None]
    width = len(key_columns)

    for start in range(0, len(ordered), LOOKUP_CHUNK):
        batch = ordered[start:start + LOOKUP_CHUNK]
        query = db.query(*(key_attrs + [a for _c, a in want_attrs]))
        if width == 1:
            query = query.filter(key_attrs[0].in_([k[0] for k in batch]))
        else:
            # Composite key: one OR-of-ANDs per chunk. Still one statement per chunk.
            from sqlalchemy import and_, or_
            query = query.filter(or_(*[
                and_(*[key_attrs[i] == part for i, part in enumerate(k)])
                for k in batch]))

        try:
            rows = _isolated_fetch(db, query)
        except Exception as e:
            # Named, loud, and scoped. The session is alive because the SAVEPOINT was
            # rolled back; these keys will say `mapping_unavailable` and the rest of
            # the batch is unaffected.
            unreadable.update(batch)
            logger.error(
                "[CrossTableLookup] reference read on '%s' failed for a chunk of keys "
                "(%s: %s); those rows will report %s",
                table, type(e).__name__, e, REASON_UNREADABLE)
            continue

        for row in rows:
            index[tuple(row[:width])] = {
                c: row[width + i] for i, (c, _a) in enumerate(want_attrs)}

    return index, unreadable


def _as_number(value):
    """A reference value this rule can actually compute with, or None."""
    try:
        return float(value)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


# --- The mapper ------------------------------------------------------------------

def build_pack_weight_batch(db: Session, payloads, rule=None) -> dict:
    """Derive one target row per trigger row, using values read from ANOTHER table.

    RULE A, stated once and visible everywhere below: THIS FUNCTION NEVER COMMITS, NEVER
    ROLLS BACK, AND NEVER CLOSES THE SESSION. The worker opens one session per polling
    batch and shares it across every transaction group in that batch. A `db.commit()`
    here would end that transaction at an arbitrary point, which means (a) anything the
    group had pending escapes the failure path's `db.rollback()`, so the group is still
    retried and the escaped part is applied twice, and (b) `expire_on_commit` invalidates
    every ORM object the worker is holding, including the outbox rows it is about to
    stamp, so the stamping reloads them one statement at a time.
    """
    # `is_batch: false` hands over a single dict. Accepted, because both shapes are
    # legal per the worker - but warned about, because that setting makes the N+1 this
    # file is built to avoid STRUCTURAL: the mapper is called once per event, so the
    # reference is read once per row no matter how well the read is written.
    if isinstance(payloads, dict):
        logger.warning(
            "[CrossTableLookup] rule '%s' called with a single payload; declare "
            "`is_batch: true` or the reference is read once per row.",
            (rule or {}).get("name"))
        payloads = [payloads]
    if not payloads:
        return {"updates": []}

    target_table = _rule_str(rule, "target_table", "")
    lookup_table = _rule_str(rule, "lookup_table", DEFAULT_LOOKUP_TABLE)
    factor_columns = _rule_tuple(rule, "lookup_value_columns", DEFAULT_FACTOR_COLUMNS)
    frozen_columns = _rule_tuple(rule, "frozen_columns", DEFAULT_FROZEN_COLUMNS)
    computed_column = _rule_str(rule, "computed_column", DEFAULT_COMPUTED_COLUMN)
    status_column = _rule_str(rule, "status_column", DEFAULT_STATUS_COLUMN)
    key_column = _rule_str(rule, "target_business_key_column", DEFAULT_TARGET_KEY_COLUMN)

    rows = [_flatten(p) for p in payloads]

    try:
        pairs = _lookup_pairs(rule)
        if len(frozen_columns) != len(factor_columns):
            # They are zipped below, and `zip` truncates in silence. A rule that froze
            # some of its inputs and not others would produce a row that looks fully
            # audited and is not.
            raise LookupRefused(
                REFUSE_TARGET_COLUMN_UNDECLARED,
                "`frozen_columns` %r does not pair up with `lookup_value_columns` %r; "
                "every value this rule computes with has to be recorded next to what "
                "it produced." % (list(frozen_columns), list(factor_columns)))
        _require_declared_target_columns(
            target_table,
            [computed_column, status_column] + list(frozen_columns))

        # De-duplicate before reading. Many trigger rows usually point at one
        # reference record, and asking for the same key repeatedly is the per-row
        # shape wearing a batch's clothes.
        wanted_keys = set()
        for row in rows:
            key = tuple(row.get(left) for left, _right in pairs)
            if any(k is None or str(k).strip() == "" for k in key):
                continue      # an incomplete key selects nothing, never everything
            wanted_keys.add(key)

        index, unreadable = load_reference(
            db, lookup_table,
            [right for _left, right in pairs], list(factor_columns), wanted_keys)
    except LookupRefused as e:
        # Empty-handed and NAMED. A partially derived batch is not a smaller claim than
        # a complete one, it is a different claim.
        #
        # Returning rather than raising is a decision, and it is the one `dt_map_mapper`
        # makes too. Every refusal above is a DECLARATION error: it will be identical on
        # every retry, so raising would burn the group's retry budget and quarantine the
        # trigger events as FAILED without producing any information the first attempt
        # did not already have. The error log is the signal. Raise instead when the
        # cause could plausibly be gone by the next attempt.
        logger.error("[CrossTableLookup] rule '%s' refused: %s",
                     (rule or {}).get("name"), e)
        return {"updates": []}

    from database import crud

    updates = []
    for row in rows:
        business_key = row.get(key_column)
        if crud.is_blank_value(business_key):
            # Nothing to address the derived row WITH. This one really is a skip: an
            # update item with no key would either create an unaddressable row or land
            # on an arbitrary one.
            logger.warning(
                "[CrossTableLookup] trigger row %s carries no '%s'; not derived.",
                row.get("row_id"), key_column)
            continue

        cells = {key_column: business_key}
        key = tuple(row.get(left) for left, _right in pairs)

        # RULE D. The row SAYS which outcome it got. Nothing here guesses a value and
        # nothing drops a row for not knowing one - a derived row that omits both the
        # value and the reason is indistinguishable from one that was never attempted.
        #
        # KNOW THE LIMIT OF ONE STATUS COLUMN: it can carry the answer about one
        # subject, and here that subject is the REFERENCE. A trigger row that arrived
        # without a usable key never made it into `wanted_keys`, so it lands here as
        # not-found and is reported `not_declared` - true (no reference was located)
        # but pointing at the wrong end of the join. If your rule can fail to know
        # about more than one subject, give each subject its own status column;
        # `dt_map_derivation` splits its hold reasons for exactly that reason, because
        # they are different repairs.
        found = index.get(key)
        if key in unreadable:
            status = REASON_UNREADABLE
        elif not found:
            status = REASON_ABSENT
        else:
            # A blank reference cell and an unusable one are different repairs, so
            # they get different words: go fill the value in, versus go look at why
            # that value is not a number. Unusable is checked first because it is the
            # louder fact - a blank cell is a normal state of a table being filled in.
            blank = [c for c in factor_columns if crud.is_blank_value(found.get(c))]
            unusable = [c for c in factor_columns
                        if c not in blank and _as_number(found.get(c)) is None]
            if unusable:
                status = REASON_UNREADABLE
            elif blank:
                status = REASON_ABSENT
            else:
                status = None
                product = 1.0
                for column in factor_columns:
                    product *= _as_number(found.get(column))
                cells[computed_column] = product
                # RULE 2 of "when this is the right tool": the frozen value is written
                # NEXT TO what it produced. Without it, a later correction on the
                # reference table leaves a stored number that nobody can audit - the
                # cell is stale by design and looks exactly like a cell that is wrong.
                for column, source_column in zip(frozen_columns, factor_columns):
                    cells[column] = found.get(source_column)

        if status is not None:
            cells[status_column] = status

        # `source_name` is what the layering invariant reads. `chain_ingestion` ranks
        # below `user`, so a human correction on a derived cell survives the next
        # derivation. Do not invent a source name here - an unregistered one falls to
        # the bottom rank and stops being comparable with the others.
        updates.append({
            "business_key_val": business_key,
            "updates": cells,
            "source_name": "chain_ingestion",
            "updated_by": "chain_worker",
        })

    # No `silent` key: see DISAGREEMENT in the module docstring. The worker builds the
    # batch itself and would ignore it.
    return {"updates": updates}
